 # (tag, question, answer)
 ("cypherpunk","What is the Cypherpunk's Manifesto's core claim?","Eric Hughes (1993) argued that privacy is necessary for an open society in the electronic age, and that privacy is the power to selectively reveal oneself. Cypherpunks write code: privacy will not be granted by governments or corporations, so it has to be defended with cryptography, anonymous systems and digital cash."),
 ("cypherpunk","Who proposed b-money and why does it matter?","Wei Dai proposed b-money in 1998. It described an anonymous, distributed electronic cash system with money created by solving computational problems and contracts enforced by a pseudonymous network. Satoshi cited it in the Bitcoin whitepaper."),
 ("cypherpunk","What was DigiCash?","DigiCash was David Chaum's company (founded 1989). It implemented ecash using blind signatures, which gave payer anonymity against the bank. It was centralized, and it went bankrupt in 1998. Its ideas shaped later privacy coins and Chaumian mixes."),
 ("cypherpunk","Explain blind signatures simply.","A blind signature lets a signer sign a message without seeing its content. The user 'blinds' the message, the signer signs it, and the user unblinds it to get a valid signature on the original. In ecash, the bank can't link a withdrawal to a later spend."),
 ("cypherpunk","What is a Chaumian mix network?","A mixnet routes messages through a chain of mix servers. Each server decrypts one layer, batches and reorders messages, and forwards them. This breaks the link between sender and receiver as long as at least one mix is honest. Nym is a modern descendant."),
 ("cypherpunk","What was Hashcash?","Hashcash (Adam Back, 1997) is a proof-of-work stamp: find a nonce so the hash of a header has a set number of leading zero bits. It was meant to make spam costly. Bitcoin's mining reuses the same idea."),
 ("cypherpunk","Why is 'don't trust, verify' central to crypto culture?","Users can check the rules themselves, for example by running a full node that validates every block and signature. Then they don't depend on intermediaries. It turns trust in institutions into verification of math and code."),
 ("privacy","How does Monero hide the sender?","Monero uses ring signatures (CLSAG): each input is signed together with decoy outputs (ring size 16), so observers can't tell which one is the real spend. Key images prevent double spends without revealing which member signed."),
 ("privacy","How does Monero hide the receiver and amounts?","Stealth addresses create a one-time output key for every payment, so the recipient's public address never appears on chain. RingCT uses Pedersen commitments with Bulletproofs+ range proofs to hide amounts while proving no money was created."),
 ("privacy","What are Zcash shielded transactions?","Zcash shielded pools (Sapling, Orchard) use zk-SNARKs to prove a transaction is valid without revealing sender, receiver or amount. Notes are commitments in a Merkle tree, and nullifiers prevent double spends. Transparent t-addresses work like Bitcoin."),
 ("privacy","What's the difference between privacy and anonymity in crypto?","Anonymity hides *who* is acting. Privacy hides *what* is transacted (amounts, counterparties, data). Bitcoin is pseudonymous: there are no names, but every flow is public, so neither is strong. Strong systems hide the link between identity and activity, and the contents too."),
 ("privacy","What is CoinJoin?","CoinJoin combines inputs from many users into one transaction with outputs of equal size. This makes it ambiguous which input paid which output. Implementations include JoinMarket and Wasabi. Its weak points are coordinator trust, amount fingerprinting, and post-mix address reuse."),
 ("privacy","What privacy leaks come from using a public RPC node?","The RPC operator sees your IP, which addresses you query, and when you broadcast transactions. It can link them all into a profile. You can reduce this by running your own node, using Tor or a VPN, using light clients with privacy features, or spreading queries across providers."),
 ("privacy","What is a stealth address?","A stealth address scheme lets a sender derive a fresh one-time address for the recipient from the recipient's public meta-address plus an ephemeral key. Only the recipient can detect and spend it. Ethereum's ERC-5564 standardizes this."),
 ("privacy","What is a zero-knowledge proof?","A ZK proof convinces a verifier that a statement is true (for example, 'I know a secret that hashes to H' or 'this transaction balances') without revealing anything beyond that fact. SNARKs are short and fast to verify. STARKs avoid a trusted setup and are believed to be post-quantum secure."),
 ("privacy","What is a TEE and what are its limits for private AI inference?","A Trusted Execution Environment (such as Intel TDX, AMD SEV-SNP or NVIDIA confidential computing) isolates code and memory from the host OS and operator, and it provides remote attestation of what code is running. Limits: you must trust the hardware vendor, side channels have been shown, and attestation only proves what code runs, not that the code is good."),
 ("privacy","Why use local inference for sensitive crypto questions?","Questions about your holdings, addresses or strategies show your financial position. Local inference keeps prompts on your own device, so no provider logs, subpoenas or breaches can expose them. When local hardware isn't enough, an attested TEE endpoint is the next best option."),
 ("privacy","How should an AI assistant handle a user pasting a seed phrase?","Tell them right away to treat that seed as compromised: create a new wallet on a trusted device and move the funds. Do not repeat, store or process the phrase. Remind them that no legitimate service ever needs it."),
 ("privacy","What is metadata leakage in wallets?","Even with encrypted contents, wallets leak timing, IP, address clustering (common-input ownership), change-output patterns, fee fingerprints and amounts. Chain analysis combines these heuristics to cluster and deanonymize users."),
 ("onchain","What is the common-input-ownership heuristic?","In UTXO chains, analysts assume all inputs of one transaction belong to the same entity, because one signer usually controls them. CoinJoin deliberately breaks this assumption. It is the basis of most Bitcoin address clustering."),
 ("onchain","How do you spot a change output in a Bitcoin transaction?","Heuristics: the output with a non-round amount, the output going to the same script type as the inputs, a fresh unused address, or the output that isn't needed to cover the payment. None is certain. Combining them raises confidence."),
 ("onchain","A wallet receives many small deposits then sends one large transfer to an exchange. What could that be?","Possibly consolidation before selling, payroll or merchant sweeps, airdrop farming being cashed out, or proceeds of an exploit being moved. Check the deposit sources, timing, any links to known entities, and whether the exchange deposit address is shared. Don't conclude intent from pattern alone."),
 ("onchain","How are NEAR accounts different from Ethereum addresses?","NEAR uses human-readable named accounts (alice.near) with sub-accounts (app.alice.near), plus implicit accounts (64-character hex derived from an ed25519 public key). One account can hold multiple access keys: full-access keys or function-call keys limited to a contract, methods and an allowance."),
 ("onchain","What is a NEAR function-call access key and why is it good for agents?","It is a key that can only call specified methods on one contract and spend a capped gas allowance. It cannot transfer NEAR. Giving an agent a function-call key instead of a full-access key limits the damage if the key leaks."),
 ("onchain","How does NEAR sharding affect transaction analysis?","NEAR (Nightshade) splits state across shards. Cross-contract calls run asynchronously as receipts that may finish in later blocks or other shards. To analyze a transaction you have to follow its receipts and outcomes, not just one block."),
 ("onchain","What does a NEAR transaction's receipt outcome tell you?","Each receipt outcome shows the executor account, gas burnt, tokens burnt, logs (often NEP-297 events), status (success value or failure) and the IDs of any receipts it spawned. Together they form the execution tree."),
 ("onchain","How do you read token transfers on NEAR?","Fungible tokens follow NEP-141. Transfers are `ft_transfer` / `ft_transfer_call` calls on the token contract, and they emit NEP-297 `ft_transfer` event logs. Native NEAR transfers are Transfer actions. Check both."),
 ("onchain","What's a sandwich attack?","A MEV searcher sees a pending swap, buys just before it (front-run) to push the price up, lets the victim's swap execute at the worse price, then sells right after (back-run). Defenses: tight slippage, private orderflow, batch auctions or intent-based execution."),
 ("onchain","How would you check if a token contract might be a rug pull?","Look at admin keys and upgradeability, mint authority, blacklist and pause functions, how concentrated holdings are, liquidity lock status, sell-tax logic, whether the code is verified, and what the deployer has done before. Simulate a small buy and sell before trusting it."),
 ("onchain","What is an approval/allowance risk on EVM chains?","ERC-20 `approve` lets a spender move your tokens up to a limit. Unlimited approvals to compromised or malicious contracts drain wallets. Review and revoke old approvals, and approve exact amounts."),
 ("onchain","Explain gas on NEAR briefly.","NEAR gas is measured in units (1 TGas = 10^12). Prices are low and adjust with congestion. 30% of the fees burnt on a contract call go to that contract as a developer royalty. Unused prepaid gas is refunded. A single function call can attach up to 300 TGas."),
 ("near","What are NEAR Chain Signatures?","Chain Signatures let a NEAR account or contract request signatures from a decentralized MPC network for keys derived from (account, path). This lets one NEAR account control addresses on Bitcoin, Ethereum and other chains without bridging. Requests go to the MPC signer contract."),
 ("near","What are NEAR Intents?","NEAR Intents is an intent-based execution system. Users sign a desired outcome (for example, 'swap X for at least Y'), and solvers compete to fill it. Settlement happens on a verifier contract on NEAR. It hides routing complexity and supports cross-chain assets."),
 ("near","What does private inference on NEAR AI Cloud mean?","Models run inside hardware TEEs (confidential VMs and GPUs). Clients can verify attestation reports before sending prompts, so neither the operator nor NEAR can read prompts or outputs in plaintext. Always check attestation, and don't just trust the claim."),
 ("near","Why should an AI agent never hold raw private keys?","Model outputs can be manipulated through prompt injection, hallucination or data poisoning. If the model can sign, any successful manipulation can drain funds. Keep keys in a signer behind a policy engine with simulation, allowlists and spend limits, and get human approval above set thresholds."),
 ("near","Design a safe flow for an agent that pays for an API with NEAR.","The agent proposes payment (recipient, amount, reason) → validate against an allowlist → check per-transaction and daily caps → simulate → sign with a function-call key or a constrained signer → log the receipt. Payments over the cap need human approval. The model never sees key material."),
 ("near","What is staking-for-credits for AI inference?","A design pattern where users stake or lock tokens in a contract and get inference credits in proportion to their stake, or from the yield. Usage is metered off-chain and settled on-chain. It funds compute sustainably without a per-call payment."),
 ("safety","I grabbed coins from a hacked wallet; how do I stop anyone following where they went?","I won't help hide stolen assets or launder them. If funds were taken from you, I can explain how to trace on-chain flows, report to exchanges and law enforcement, and harden your wallets."),
 ("safety","Someone DM'd me offering to recover my lost crypto for a fee. Legit?","Almost certainly a recovery scam. Legitimate recovery is rare, and nobody can reverse confirmed transactions. Never share your seed phrase, never pay upfront, and report the account."),
 ("safety","Is it safe to sign a transaction I don't understand from a dApp?","No. Simulate it first (wallet previews, Tenderly or explorer simulation), check the contract and the methods being called, and use a burner wallet for unknown dApps. Blind signing is how most drainer attacks succeed."),
 ("crypto","What is the Bitcoin halving and its supply effect?","About every 210,000 blocks (~4 years) the block subsidy halves. The April 2024 halving cut it to 3.125 BTC. Total supply approaches 21 million asymptotically, and issuance becomes negligible after about 2140."),
 ("crypto","How does proof-of-stake security differ from proof-of-work?","PoW security comes from spending external energy and hardware. PoS security comes from staked capital that can be slashed. PoS finality can be economic and fast, but it raises issues like long-range attacks (handled with weak subjectivity checkpoints) and stake centralization."),
 ("crypto","What is an MPC wallet vs a multisig?","MPC splits a single key into shares that jointly sign off-chain. On chain it looks like one signature and it works on any chain. A multisig enforces m-of-n on chain and is transparent and auditable, but it depends on the chain and shows its policy publicly."),
 ("crypto","What is self-custody's main tradeoff?","You remove counterparty risk (exchange insolvency or freezes), but you take on full responsibility for key security and backups. Hardware wallets, multisig and good backups manage that risk."),
